1. Executive thesis and market timing

1.1 The business problem both products address

The common problem is not a lack of dashboards or AI features. Organizations accumulate information in disconnected places while decisions happen across people, processes and systems. An owner may see satisfactory activity metrics in marketing, sales, operations and finance while the handoffs between those functions create duplicated work, slow approvals, missed revenue, inconsistent ownership and poor learning. Aetheris should sell a disciplined method for locating, evidencing and prioritizing those breakdowns before proposing technology.

Mindprint addresses a related but narrower question: how do available professional evidence and first-party input help an executive understand expressed priorities, decision preferences, communication patterns and assumptions worth verifying? It is best positioned as an evidence exploration and preparation tool, not mind reading, clinical psychometrics or a substitute for direct conversation.

1.2 Why these markets deserve attention

Deloitte reported that 80% of surveyed manufacturing executives intended to allocate at least 20% of improvement budgets to smart manufacturing. That indicates openness to operational technology, but not automatic demand for Aetheris. [1]

PwC found that 44% of surveyed US family businesses said succession planning affected them and 47% cited talent and leadership development. These are plausible entry points for governance and decision clarity. [2]

Gallup reported manager engagement dropped to 22% globally in 2025, indicating organizational strain; this is not evidence that behavioral mapping automatically solves it. [3]

McKinsey found that companies capturing AI value are redesigning workflows and elevating governance rather than merely deploying tools. That supports leading with operational redesign rather than AI hype. [4]

