4. Aetheris: buyer committee and pains

4.1 Economic buyer: founder, CEO or president

Internal motivation: regain control over growth, prevent margin deterioration, understand what staff cannot surface through normal status reports, and avoid another failed software purchase. Fear: an outside consultant will create meetings and a report but no decisions. Winning proof: a tightly scoped sample of observed handoff friction and a short list of changes with clear ownership.

4.2 Operational champion: COO, GM, VP Operations

Internal motivation: fewer exceptions, clearer accountability, capacity without equivalent headcount growth and reliable operating rhythms. Fear: a report that blames their team or ignores floor reality. Winning proof: actual process walk-throughs, exception counts and implementation feasibility.

4.3 Financial validator: CFO, controller, finance lead

Internal motivation: explain gross margin variance, working-capital drag, receivables timing, write-offs, forecast errors and inconsistent source data. Fear: invented ROI and unsupported “financial leak” numbers. Winning proof: traceable calculations, scenario ranges and honest distinction between observed values and hypotheses.

4.4 Technical gatekeepers and users

IT, systems administrators, sales operations, production planners and supervisors determine whether data access and practical changes are feasible. They may not sign the contract, but can block adoption. The offer should start with minimal data, clear read-only requests and no surprise integration scope.

