11.2 Budget and ROI discipline

For Aetheris, anchor value in a small set of defensible units: hours of rework avoided, quote cycle time, missed conversions, schedule-change cost, inventory exceptions or time to invoice. Use ranges and explicit assumptions. A $10,000 diagnostic is justified only if the buyer values the decision and the cost of not knowing; do not invent a million-dollar leak from website evidence alone.

For Mindprint, contribution margin must include research calls, generation tokens, caching, cloud storage, checkout fees, refunds and support. At a one-time $49 product, even moderate per-profile research costs can destroy contribution margin. A $99 tier must have a visibly differentiated experience with bounded compute and user value, not simply a longer generic report.

11.3 Disqualification checklist

Is there a concrete business decision or operational process worth examining? If no, do not upsell a deep diagnostic.

Is there an authorized sponsor and access to relevant source material? If no, offer a narrow public-signal preview only.

Can the deliverable change an action, meeting or priority? If no, the buyer may be a curiosity customer.

Is the requested use ethically and legally acceptable? If no, decline that use.

Is the buyer seeking a guaranteed financial result or hidden psychological conclusion? If yes, reset expectations or disqualify.

11.4 Four highest-leverage immediate decisions

Make US founder-led precision manufacturers the primary Aetheris prospect pool; prioritize workflows rather than headcount alone.

Make founders and repeat-use executive advisers the first Mindprint purchasers; postpone large institutional HR sales until safeguards, consent and case studies are established.

Make Golden Report a focused decision-friction deliverable, not a catalog of unrelated observations.

Make Mindprint a decision-preparation tool and visibly communicate source quality, boundaries and unknowns.

